{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Discovered from\n\nrebar-debug on bug `itchy-arbored-peccary` (3907-0110-7688-43bd), while verifying a handed-in\nclaim that the janitor had no caller. It has one — but exactly one.\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`start_background_janitor` has a single production invocation: the review-bot's FastAPI\nlifespan (`src/rebar/review_bot/app.py:134-136`). So the janitor — the ONLY thing that\nreclaims `$REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR/rebar-gate-snapshots` — runs only inside the long-lived\nreview-bot server process. Every other host that resolves an attested gate (a developer\nlaptop running `rebar review-plan` / `review-code` / `verify-completion`, a CI runner, any\nlibrary embedding that is not the review-bot app) populates the snapshot store and never\nreclaims it. Measured on one developer host: 64,021 entries, 47.24 GiB, nothing ever\nreclaimed. Bug 3907 fixed the *policy* (the `max_bytes` byte-total cap); this ticket is the\n*cadence* — on a non-review-bot host there is no pass to apply the policy.\n\n## Operator ruling (design constraint)\n\nRecorded verbatim in the comment below: an operation-linked GC trigger is acceptable, BUT it\nmust avoid the long-lock pitfall we hit before (bug `0d15-59a4` — a sweep holding the ticket\nstore lock for minutes), by using the same pattern that fixed it: a near-free in-process\ncheck on the tail of the operation, heavy work in a DETACHED child, stamp/lock sidecars for\ncadence and single-flight.\n\n## Approach\n\nMirror `src/rebar/_commands/compact_trigger.py` — the compaction sweep's operation-linked\ntrigger, the pattern that fixed 0d15-59a4 — applied to the EXISTING snapshot-GC policy\n(`janitor.run_gc`: hysteretic watermark, grace window, cold-trim, `max_bytes` backstop).\n\nNew module `src/rebar/_snapshot/gc_trigger.py`:\n\n- **Sidecars keyed on the snapshot store root** (`<store>/gc/last-pass.stamp` and\n  `<store>/gc/worker.lock`), not on any repo/worktree path — the store is per-host and\n  already canonical, so two worktrees of one repo share one clock and one lock (the\n  dae7f62696 discipline, applied where this store actually lives).\n- **`maybe_gc(repo_root)` — the operation-linked trigger.** Called on the tail of gate\n  resolution (`gate_source.resolve_gate_handle`, the single choke point every attested gate\n  op flows through — review-plan, verify-completion, review-code, scan-spec, workflows), for\n  attested handles only. Its decision is O(1): one `stat` of the stamp (a02eedbb6e's marker\n  fast-path discipline — no store enumeration, nothing that grows with entry count) against\n  the janitor's existing `interval_seconds` cadence knob (`<= 0` disables the trigger). It\n  takes NO ticket-store lock — it never imports or touches `rebar._store.lock` — and never\n  raises (never fails the gate that triggered it).\n- **Detached child** (`_spawn_detached_gc`, mirroring `_spawn_detached_sweep`): PYTHONPATH\n  bootstrap, stdio to a log under `<store>/gc/`, durable cwd anchored at the snapshot store\n  root (the d575600381 concern: never inherit an ephemeral worktree cwd), POSIX-only v1\n  (win32 no-op, exactly like compact_trigger and enrich_drain).\n- **Single-flight**: the trigger probes `worker.lock` (an `O_EXCL` file carrying the v2\n  owner stamp, adjudicated by `lock_owner.stamped_file_is_stale` — pid-recycle\n  qualification and stale reclamation inherited, not re-invented) before spawning, and the\n  child re-acquires it for real. `run_gc` additionally holds its own non-blocking `flock` on\n  `<store>/gc/lock`, so overlap with the review-bot's resident janitor is harmless by\n  construction (the loser returns `skipped=\"locked\"`).\n- **The stamp records only an actual pass** (the `run_sweep` lesson: a stand-aside must not\n  reset the clock): the child stamps `last-pass.stamp` only when `run_gc` ran\n  (`skipped is None`). The review-bot's resident janitor is untouched and keeps running;\n  because its passes go through the same `run_gc`, hosts where it runs simply see fresh\n  passes and the op-linked trigger stays quiet.\n\nADR 0005 D5 (\"never invoked from populate/read\") holds in spirit and is amended with a note:\nthe trigger check on the gate-resolution tail is one stat, and the pass itself runs in a\ndetached child off every hot path.\n\nPortability (`project.portability`): the trigger is operation-linked and in-process — it\nrequires no CI provider, no cron, no daemon; the review-bot's resident thread remains a\nsupplementary cadence, not the floor.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_snapshot/gc_trigger.py` (new): stamp/lock sidecar helpers, `maybe_gc`,\n  `_spawn_detached_gc`, `run_detached` (the child entry point).\n- `src/rebar/_snapshot/__init__.py`: export `maybe_gc`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/gate_source.py`: call `maybe_gc` on the tail of `resolve_gate_handle`\n  (attested handles only, never-raise).\n- `tests/unit/test_snapshot_gc_trigger.py` (new).\n- `docs/repo-snapshot-gates.md`: document when a pass runs on a non-review-bot host and how\n  an operator forces one (run the trigger's child entry point / set the interval).\n- `docs/adr/0005-snapshot-cache-architecture.md`: D5 amendment note.\n\n## Testing\n\nRED-first (tests written and failing before the implementation), mirroring\n`tests/unit/test_compact_trigger.py`'s style (monkeypatched spawn capture; fake-`Popen`\nargv capture for the detach contract):\n\n- **Trigger fires**: with a stale/missing stamp, `maybe_gc` spawns the detached GC; a\n  gate-resolution wiring test asserts `resolve_gate_handle` invokes the trigger for an\n  attested handle and not for a local one.\n- **Near-free + lock-free on the quiet path**: with a fresh stamp, `maybe_gc` performs no\n  directory enumeration of the store (does not scale with entry count) and touches no\n  ticket-store lock (the store-lock acquire is patched to fail loudly).\n- **Single-flight under contention**: a held `worker.lock` suppresses the spawn; a live\n  lock is respected, a provably-stale one is reclaimed; overlap with a concurrent `run_gc`\n  degrades to `skipped=\"locked\"`.\n- **Reclamation on a simulated non-review-bot host (the headline AC)**: a store with a cold\n  entry and no resident janitor, spawn patched to run the child entry point inline →\n  the entry is evicted and the stamp records the pass; a second immediate trigger stays\n  quiet (failure/empty path: fresh stamp → no spawn, nothing evicted).\n- **Detach contract**: fake `Popen` captures argv/kwargs — the child is handed the resolved\n  store root, cwd is the durable store root, stdio is detached.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] A host that runs attested gates WITHOUT the review-bot server reclaims its snapshot\n      store: a gate resolution on a due stamp spawns a detached `run_gc` pass.\n- [x] The trigger check is O(1) on the quiet path — no store enumeration, no ticket-store\n      lock, never raises into the gate — and the GC pass itself runs in a detached child\n      off the gate's critical path (ADR 0005 D5 amended with the reasoning).\n- [x] The trigger is operation-linked and in-process — not dependent on any CI provider,\n      cron, or daemon (`project.portability`).\n- [x] Single-flight across simultaneous hosts/worktrees: stamped worker-lock sidecar +\n      `run_gc`'s existing gc flock; double-running with the review-bot's resident janitor\n      is harmless (`skipped=\"locked\"`), and the resident janitor is not regressed.\n- [x] A test observes reclamation happening on a simulated non-review-bot host — RED before\n      the change, GREEN after.\n- [x] `docs/repo-snapshot-gates.md` documents when a pass runs and how an operator forces\n      one."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787342248075273001,"uuid":"1254fae9-a49f-4334-8111-3bde2b4d42ac"}